        "math_academy_id": "q-113827",
        "problem": "Consider the following polynomial division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5x-3 \\\\  & x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,5{x}^{2}+2x-6\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5{x}^{2}+5x}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3x-6 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-3x-3}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x-6}{x+1}=$",
        "worked_solution": "$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5x-3 \\\\  & x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,5{x}^{2}+2x-6\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5{x}^{2}+5x}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3x-6 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-3x-3}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFrom the given polynomial division, we deduce that dividing the polynomial $5{x}^{2}+2x-6$ by the linear binomial $x+1$ gives\n\n- the quotient $5x-3,$ and\n\n- the remainder $-3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x-6}{x+1}=5x-3-\\frac{3}{x+1}.\n$$",
